    {
      "id": "HI-S141-nuqta-borrowed",
      "sequence": 2265,
      "title": "ज़ फ़ ख़ क़ (za fa xa qa) — the same dot, a different job",
      "headword": "ज़ फ़ ख़ क़",
      "romanization": "za fa xa qa",
      "gloss": "the four dotted letters that carry sounds Hindi borrowed — the same dot, doing a different job",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "pen",
      "derivedModality": "pen",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "writing-type",
        "writing-block",
        "script-block"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:8798dd916b74a4fb",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "pen",
        "needs": [
          "a pen and something to write on",
          "your eyes, for letter shapes on the page"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Script you'll notice: ज़ फ़ ख़ क़",
          "Script: words you already say",
          "Guided Practice"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your hands, so it is not a driving lesson. You will want a pen and something to write on and your eyes, for letter shapes on the page. You can listen to everything else now — leave the sections called Script you'll notice: ज़ फ़ ख़ क़, Script: words you already say and Guided Practice until you have stopped, and I will say so again when we reach it."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The mark from before: where does it sit, and what does it change?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "On ड and ढ it made a flap. On these four it does something else — it lets the letter carry a sound that came into Hindi from outside it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "Script you'll notice: ज़ फ़ ख़ क़",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "letter",
                "with the dot",
                "sound"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 4,
              "utterances": [
                "letter: ज. with the dot: ज़. sound: za.",
                "letter: फ. with the dot: फ़. sound: fa.",
                "letter: ख. with the dot: ख़. sound: xa.",
                "letter: क. with the dot: क़. sound: qa."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ज़ is the z of English zoo. फ़ is the f of far. ख़ is made further back than ख, at the soft palate. क़ is made further back than क, at the very top of the throat."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Many speakers say the plain letter for all four and are understood. The dot is still printed, so a reader has to know it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "script",
          "title": "Script: words you already say",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ज़रूर — certainly, मेज़ — table, दरवाज़ा — door."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सफ़ेद — white, सफ़र — a journey, तोहफ़ा — a gift."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ख़ुशी — happiness, ख़ुशबू — fragrance."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "मुलाक़ात — a meeting."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One pair you have met already. खाना and ख़ाना were taught together, and the dot between them was named there as a fact about those two words. It is the same dot, and it is doing the same job in every word on this page. That lesson also told you the truth about it: the dot is often left off in print, and a reader sorts the two out from context."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "writing",
          "title": "Writing: the dotted four — copy what you see",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The letter is drawn exactly as you draw it bare. One dot goes below, clear of the line."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put your pen on ज़ and follow it, then फ़. Copy slowly and larger than it is printed."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This book does not tell you where to start the character or which way to travel. That is a real thing, taught with real variation from school to school, and it is not written down here until it can be written down with a source."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "LOOK",
              "instruction": "at these two words and say which one means food",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU LOOK: at these two words and say which one means food]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "खाना, ख़ाना."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "ज़रूर and मेज़ aloud, letting the dotted letter buzz like z",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: ज़रूर and मेज़ aloud, letting the dotted letter buzz like *z*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "TRACE",
              "instruction": "फ़ three times, saying fa as you finish each one",
              "spoken": false,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU TRACE: फ़ three times, saying *fa* as you finish each one]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 5,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which four letters take the dot for a borrowed sound? Name the sound each one carries. What tells खाना from ख़ाना on the page?"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
